L["jacob-dream"] = """Jacob was the younger of Isaac and Rebekah's twin sons. His brother Esau was born first, which meant Esau should have gotten the family blessing. But Jacob, whose name means he grabs the heel, or he cheats, got both the birthright and the blessing by trickery. He bought the birthright for a bowl of stew. Then, with his mother's help, he dressed up as Esau to fool his blind father into giving him the blessing.

Esau was furious and planned to kill him. Rebekah heard about it and sent Jacob away to her brother Laban, far to the north in Haran. Jacob was running for his life. He was leaving the only home he'd ever known, with no one to go with him. He was alone, and he had done wrong.

At sundown, he reached a certain place and stopped for the night. He took one of the stones there and put it under his head and lay down. He was in the middle of nowhere, at one of the lowest points of his life.

Then he had a dream. He saw a stairway resting on the earth, with its top reaching to heaven, and the angels of God were going up and down on it. At the top stood the Lord. He said, I am the Lord, the God of your father Abraham and the God of Isaac. I will give you and your descendants the land on which you are lying. All peoples on earth will be blessed through you and your offspring.

Then came the line that matters most. I am with you and will watch over you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land. I will not leave you until I have done what I have promised you.

Think about what that meant. Jacob had cheated his brother and lied to his father. God didn't say, you have to fix your life first. He said, I am with you. He kept the promise he had made to Jacob's grandfather, and he put it into the hands of a man who did not deserve it.

Jacob woke up and said, surely the Lord is in this place, and I was not aware of it. He was afraid. How awesome is this place! This is none other than the house of God. This is the gate of heaven. Early in the morning he took the stone he had used as a pillow, set it up as a pillar, and poured oil on top of it. He named the place Bethel, which means house of God.

Then he made a vow. If God would be with him and look after him and give him food and clothes, then the Lord would be his God. It was a halting, bargaining prayer, but it was a start.

Why does this matter? This is the first time Jacob meets God for himself. Until now he had only heard about the God of Abraham and Isaac. Now he knew God was his God too. And it shows that God meets us in the worst places, often when we least expect it.

Jesus made a reference to this dream. When he met Nathanael, he told him, you will see heaven open, and the angels of God ascending and descending on the Son of Man. Jesus was saying that he is the stairway. He is where heaven and earth meet.

What happened after? Jacob went on to Haran. There he worked for his uncle for twenty years, and the man who had been a trickster was tricked himself. He married Leah and Rachel and had twelve sons, who became the twelve tribes of Israel. And God did bring him home, as he said.

What does this mean for us today? You do not have to be a good person to have God come to you. Jacob was running away, with a guilty conscience and a rock for a pillow. And God showed up. That is grace.

It also means that places we think of as empty may be holy ground. Jacob said, the Lord is in this place and I did not know it. Maybe God is in the hospital waiting room, the hard season, or the strange town where you ended up.

That is why this story is so important. It shows a God who does not wait for us to clean up before he comes close. He said to Jacob, I am with you, and he says it to us too."""

L["jacob-wrestles"] = """Twenty years had passed since Jacob ran away from home. He had worked for his uncle Laban in Haran, married two wives, had eleven sons and a daughter, and built up a large herd. Now God told him to go home. But going home meant facing his brother Esau, whom he had cheated and who had once threatened to kill him.

Jacob sent messengers ahead to Esau. They came back with bad news. Esau is coming to meet you, and he has four hundred men with him. Jacob was terrified. He divided his people and flocks into two groups so that if one was attacked, the other might escape. Then he prayed. It is one of the most honest prayers in the Bible. He reminded God of his promises. I am unworthy of all the kindness you have shown me. Save me, I pray, from the hand of my brother Esau. For I am afraid he will come and attack me, and the mothers with their children.

He then picked out a large gift for Esau, hundreds of goats, sheep, camels, cattle, and donkeys, and sent them ahead in droves, each with a servant. He hoped to soften his brother before he saw him. That night he sent his family across the Jabbok River with all he had. And Jacob was left alone.

Then a man wrestled with him until daybreak. The Bible doesn't explain at first who this was. Jacob was strong, and the man could not overpower him. So the man touched the socket of Jacob's hip, and it was wrenched as they wrestled. Then he said, let me go, for it is daybreak. But Jacob said, I will not let you go unless you bless me.

The man asked, what is your name? Jacob. And the man said, your name will no longer be Jacob, but Israel, because you have struggled with God and with humans and have overcome. Israel means he struggles with God. Jacob asked for his name, but the man would only bless him there.

Jacob named the place Peniel, which means face of God. It is because I saw God face to face, and yet my life was spared. The sun rose as he passed Peniel, and he was limping because of his hip. To this day, the Bible says, the Israelites do not eat the tendon attached to the socket of the hip.

Why does this matter? Look at what Jacob did. He had been running and scheming and bargaining his whole life. In this one night, he stopped. He held on to God and refused to let go until God blessed him. He came out of it with a new name and a limp. God didn't take away his struggle. He changed him in it.

The limp is the key. From then on, Jacob couldn't run away or fight in his own strength. He walked with a limp for the rest of his life, and that was a sign that he now leaned on God. The man who had always depended on himself was now broken in the right place.

What happened after? The next morning Esau came running to meet him. And, as we will see, Jacob got a very different welcome than he had been afraid of. The nation God made from him took Israel as its name.

What does this mean for us today? Some of us are afraid of something coming at us tomorrow, and are awake with it tonight. Jacob prayed, and then he wrestled. Staying up with God over the thing we're afraid of is not a lack of faith. It is faith.

It also shows that it is okay to hold on to God and say, bless me. God doesn't push us away for being persistent. Jesus told stories about people who kept knocking.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us that real change comes from being alone with God, and that God sometimes marks the people he blesses. If you have a limp from a hard season, it may be the very sign that God was there."""

L["jacob-esau"] = """Jacob had spent the night wrestling with God. Now the sun was coming up and he limped toward the ford of the Jabbok. He looked up and saw Esau coming with four hundred men. This was the moment he had feared for twenty years.

Jacob arranged his family in order. He put the maids and their children first, Leah and her children next, and Rachel and Joseph last, because they were the most precious to him. Then he walked out in front of them all, and bowed down to the ground seven times as he approached his brother. This was the way a servant would greet a king. He was the one who had been given the family blessing, and he bowed to the brother he had cheated.

But Esau ran to meet him. He threw his arms around him, hugged him, and kissed him. And they both wept. Twenty years of fear and anger were gone in a moment. The brother who had once vowed to kill Jacob was now holding him.

Esau looked up and saw the women and children. Who are these with you? Jacob said, they are the children God has graciously given your servant. One by one, the families came and bowed down to Esau.

Esau asked about the animals he'd met along the way. What did you mean by all these droves I met? Jacob said, to find favor in your eyes, my lord. Esau said, I already have plenty, my brother. Keep what you have for yourself. But Jacob insisted. Please, if I have found favor in your eyes, accept this gift from me. For to see your face is like seeing the face of God, now that you have received me favorably. He pressed him, and Esau took it.

Think of how Jacob had spent that long night. He had seen the face of God and lived. Now he saw his brother's face and said it was like seeing God's face. God's forgiveness had come first, and now it was showing up in Esau.

Esau offered to travel with him. Jacob politely said the children were tender and the flocks needed to go slowly. Let my lord go on ahead of his servant. Esau went back to Seir, to his home in the south. And Jacob went to Succoth and then settled near the city of Shechem.

Why does this matter? Forgiveness is possible even for the worst betrayals. Esau had every right to be angry. Instead, he ran. Jesus told a story about a father running to welcome his son. It sounds a lot like this one.

It also shows that Jacob had really changed. The man who once took his brother's blessing now gave a blessing back. The Hebrew word Jacob used for his gift is blessing, the same word as the one he had stolen.

What happened after? The two brothers met again once, years later, at their father Isaac's burial. They buried him together. Both of them became the fathers of nations. Esau's family became Edom, and Jacob's became Israel. The two nations would later have a hard history, but at this moment the two brothers were at peace.

What does this mean for us today? Is there someone you've wronged, or who has wronged you, and you've been dreading seeing them? Jacob dreaded it for twenty years. And what he found was that God had gone ahead of him and softened his brother's heart.

It also means that you can go first. Jacob bowed, gave his gift, and said, I'm sorry, in everything but the words. Reconciliation often needs one person to humble themselves.

That is why this story is so important. It is a picture of what the whole Bible is about. We were far from God, and we had earned his anger. But when we turn, he runs to us. He doesn't just tolerate us. He throws his arms around us and weeps for joy."""

L["joseph-sold"] = """Jacob had twelve sons by four women, and he loved one of them more than the others. Joseph was the first son of his beloved wife Rachel, born late in Jacob's life. Jacob made him a special robe. Most of us picture it as a coat of many colors, though some translations call it a long robe with sleeves. Either way, it was a sign that Joseph was the favorite. His brothers hated him and could not speak a kind word to him.

Joseph didn't help. He was seventeen, and he told his brothers about two dreams he'd had. In the first one, they were tying up bundles of grain in the field, and his bundle stood up while theirs gathered around and bowed to it. In the second, the sun, the moon, and eleven stars were bowing down to him. His brothers hated him even more. Even his father scolded him, though the Bible says Jacob kept the matter in mind.

One day, the older brothers took the flocks to a pasture near Shechem, far from home. Jacob sent Joseph to see how they were doing. A man found him wandering in the fields and told him they had moved on to Dothan. So Joseph went there.

The brothers saw him coming from far off. Here comes that dreamer, they said. Come, let's kill him and throw him into one of these cisterns. Then we'll say a ferocious animal devoured him. Then we'll see what comes of his dreams. Reuben, the oldest, tried to save him. He said, let's not take his life. Throw him into this cistern. His plan was to rescue him later and take him back to his father.

When Joseph reached them, they stripped him of his robe and threw him into an empty cistern. Then they sat down to eat. A caravan of Ishmaelites was coming from Gilead on its way to Egypt, with camels loaded with spices. Judah said, what will we gain if we kill our brother? Let's sell him to the Ishmaelites. After all, he is our brother, our own flesh and blood. They sold him for twenty shekels of silver.

They took Joseph's robe, killed a goat, and dipped it in the blood. They brought it to their father and said, we found this. Examine it to see whether it is your son's robe. Jacob recognized it and said, a ferocious animal has devoured him. He tore his clothes, put on sackcloth, and mourned for many days. All his children came to comfort him, but he refused to be comforted. In his grief he said he would go down to the grave mourning for his son.

Meanwhile, the Midianites sold Joseph in Egypt to Potiphar, one of Pharaoh's officials, the captain of the guard.

Why does this matter? It begins one of the greatest stories in the Bible, about how God can bring good out of evil. At this moment, nothing looks good. Joseph is a slave, his family is broken, and his father is in despair. But the Bible is already moving things into place. The dreams will come true, though not in the way anyone expected.

It also shows that jealousy can destroy a family. The brothers didn't plan this overnight. It grew out of years of resentment. And Jacob's own favoritism had planted it.

What happened after? Joseph went from being a slave to a prisoner, and then, many years later, to the second most powerful man in Egypt. The brothers would eventually bow before him, just like the dream said.

What does this mean for us today? If you've been betrayed by people close to you, this story says that betrayal is not the final word. God was with Joseph in the cistern, on the road, and in the house of Potiphar. He is with you in the pit too.

And if you are the one who has been envious, notice where it led. A few minutes of hatred changed a family for twenty years.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that nothing is wasted in God's hands. Joseph's worst day became the first step to saving his whole family. Later he said, you intended to harm me, but God intended it for good."""

L["joseph-reveals"] = """Twenty-two years had passed since the brothers sold Joseph. In that time, Joseph had gone from being a slave in Potiphar's house to a prisoner, falsely accused. In prison he interpreted dreams, and when Pharaoh had a dream no one could explain, Joseph was called. He told Pharaoh that seven years of plenty were coming and then seven years of famine. He suggested that Pharaoh find a wise man to store grain. Pharaoh put Joseph himself in charge. He was thirty years old, and second in command over all of Egypt.

The famine hit not just Egypt but the whole region. Back in Canaan, Jacob and his family were starving. Jacob sent ten of his sons to Egypt to buy grain. Benjamin, the youngest and the only other son of Rachel, stayed home. They came before the governor of the land, and bowed with their faces to the ground, just like in Joseph's dream. They didn't recognize him. He looked Egyptian, he spoke through an interpreter, and they thought he was dead. But Joseph knew them right away.

He tested them. He accused them of being spies, threw them in prison for three days, and kept Simeon as a hostage. He told them to bring their youngest brother back, or they'd never see him again. On the second trip, he had a silver cup planted in Benjamin's sack, and then said that the one with the cup would become his slave. The brothers were terrified.

This was the real test. Years before, they had sold one favored son and walked away. Now they had the chance to do it again. Judah, who had suggested selling Joseph, stepped forward. He begged the governor to take him as a slave instead. He said that if the boy didn't come home, their father would die of grief. Please let your servant remain as your slave in place of the boy. The man who had sold his brother was now ready to give up his own life for his brother.

Then Joseph could not control himself any longer. He sent all the Egyptians out of the room, and he wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard him. He said to his brothers, I am Joseph! Is my father still living? But his brothers couldn't answer him, because they were terrified.

Joseph said, come close to me. When they did, he said, I am your brother Joseph, the one you sold into Egypt. And now, do not be distressed and do not be angry with yourselves for selling me here, because it was to save lives that God sent me ahead of you. For two years now there has been famine in the land, and for the next five years there will be no plowing and reaping. God sent me ahead of you to preserve for you a remnant on earth. So then, it was not you who sent me here, but God.

He threw his arms around his brother Benjamin and wept, and then he kissed all his brothers and wept over them. After that, his brothers talked with him. Pharaoh was pleased, and he told Joseph to bring his whole family to live in the best part of Egypt, the land of Goshen. Joseph sent them back with wagons. When Jacob heard that Joseph was alive, he was stunned. He said, I'm convinced. My son Joseph is still alive. I will go and see him before I die.

Why does this matter? This is one of the greatest forgiveness scenes in the Bible. Joseph had every right to take revenge. He had the power to do anything he wanted. Instead, he wept, and he said that God had been working through all the pain.

The story also moves the family of Israel to Egypt, which sets up the next big event in the Bible. Jacob's family came as seventy people and, over the next four hundred years, became a nation. That is why Moses and the Exodus come next.

What happened after? Jacob came to Egypt and saw Joseph again. He lived there seventeen more years. When Jacob died, the brothers worried that Joseph would finally pay them back. He wept again and said, don't be afraid. You intended to harm me, but God intended it for good, to accomplish what is now being done, the saving of many lives.

What does this mean for us today? We almost all have something in our past that was done to us, or something we did to someone else. This story shows what real forgiveness looks like. Joseph didn't pretend it didn't hurt. He had cried for years. But he chose to see God's hand in it, and he chose not to take revenge.

It also shows that forgiveness changes the person who gives it. And the brothers changed too. Judah, who once traded a brother for silver, now offered himself for one.

That is why this story is so important. It points forward to Jesus, a rejected son who was handed over by his own people, and who then used the evil done to him to save them. He said from the cross, Father, forgive them. Joseph's story is a picture of how God works. He takes what people mean for evil and turns it into rescue."""
